quares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410808 1072 #uuid "4e50c9b4-d5fe-527c-b554-c5ae45ba5355" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410808 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is ${R}^{-1}.$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410807 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1069 "q-158080" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1071 "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1072 #uuid "6c899a77-435a-5aa3-92d4-0dc3a7b627aa" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410809 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410806 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1069 "q-157095" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1072 #uuid "6e2326cd-c939-5766-9e1e-50b935159886" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410810 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2}=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410804 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1069 "q-139796" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1072 #uuid "c8892a71-2970-5d65-891c-76f1124825ea" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410811 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410812 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410815 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410812 1062 #uuid "6b34aecb-fc33-591a-beae-813382c608f3" 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410812 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410809 13194139534118 17592186045417 true] [17592186410813 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410816 13194139534118 17592186045417 